Sources opened and retained

The most reusable opened sources were the [UN 2022 speaker record](https://digitallibrary.un.org/record/3999075), [CRS March 2022 DRC report](https://www.everycrsreport.com/files/2022-03-25_R43166_8f90645244ea5367f4d0b0dfdda0952a0b75a8e0.pdf), [UN Secretary-General report S/2022/892](https://monusco.unmissions.org/sites/default/files/sg_report_-_english_s-2022-892.pdf), [2022 US human-rights report](https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089109.html), [HRW state-of-siege investigation](https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/03/22/dr-congo-martial-law-brings-crackdown-east), [HRW Buhene accountability investigation](https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/05/23/dr-congo-no-inquiry-into-police-role-in-ethnic-killings), [IMF 2022 Article IV report](https://www.imf.org/en/publications/cr/issues/2022/07/05/democratic-republic-of-the-congo-staff-report-for-the-2022-article-iv-consultation-second-520400), [World Bank 2022 partnership framework announcement](https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/02/22/world-bank-deepens-commitment-to-building-human-capital-and-inclusive-development-in-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo), and [HRW indigenous-rights law account](https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/10/28/congos-indigenous-rights-bill-stalls).

Chapter-by-chapter gaps

- 1B: Resolve the exact institutional division of authority among the presidency, prime minister, Sacred Union factions, provincial authorities, military, and intelligence services. Likely sources: constitution, parliamentary records, CRS, International Crisis Group, Ebuteli/Congo Research Group.
- 2B: Build a dated M23/state-of-siege decision chronology and distinguish prevention, military response, diplomacy, and civilian protection. Likely sources: UN Secretary-General and Group of Experts reports, MONUSCO, EAC and Luanda-process records.
- 3B: Obtain UNJHRO incident-level evidence, detention outcomes, military-court practices, and command/accountability responses. Separate armed-group violence from state violations.
- 4B: Examine electoral commission independence, opposition access, assembly restrictions, and media pluralism during the 2023-election preparatory year. Likely sources: CENI records, CENCO/ECC observation, Carter Center, Freedom House, RSF/JED.
- 5B: Separate commodity-driven growth from policy effects; investigate budget execution, central-bank financing, mining revenues, and household welfare. Likely sources: IMF tables, finance ministry budgets, EITI, World Bank economic memoranda.
- 6B: Test delivery rather than announcements for free primary education, health access, social protection, gender policy, and indigenous-rights implementation. Likely sources: UNICEF, UNESCO, WHO, World Bank project results, national budget execution.
- 7B: This remains the most sensitive gap. Seek evidence with a direct personal-integrity nexus—assets, beneficial interests, intervention in investigations, appointments, or protection of associates. Do not convert general corruption into a personal finding. Likely sources: court records, IGF reports, EITI disclosures, procurement records, and carefully corroborated investigative reporting.
- 8B: Compare announced reforms with implementation: security-sector reform, audit follow-through, judicial compliance, budget execution, service delivery, and measurable policy outputs. Likely sources: IMF program reviews, World Bank implementation reports, UN reporting, Cour des comptes and IGF records.
